      "response": "To solve for \\( g(4) \\), we need to determine the function \\( g(x) \\) first. We know that \\( g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\) and \\( f(x) = 2x - 3 \\). Let's express \\( g(f(x)) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\]\nand\n\\[ f(x) = 2x - 3. \\]\n\nWe need to find a function \\( g(y) \\) such that when we substitute \\( f(x) \\) into \\( g \\), we get \\( g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\). To do this, we need to express \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\) where \\( y = f(x) \\). That is, we need to solve for \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\) from the equation \\( y = 2x - 3 \\).\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ y = 2x - 3 \\]\n\\[ y + 3 = 2x \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{y + 3}{2}. \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = \\frac{y + 3}{2} \\) into \\( g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\):\n\\[ g(y) = 5 - 4 \\left( \\frac{y + 3}{2} \\right). \\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[ g(y) = 5 - 2(y + 3) \\]\n\\[ g(y) = 5 - 2y - 6 \\]\n\\[ g(y) = -2y - 1. \\]\n\nSo, the function \\( g(x) \\) is:\n\\[ g(x) = -2x - 1. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( g(4) \\):\n\\[ g(4) = -2(4) - 1 \\]\n\\[ g(4) = -8 - 1 \\]\n\\[ g(4) = -9. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-9}\n\\]",
